finding: Re-review of HEAD 705efcc0: finding. 1. Unconditional CPU-history population is not earned by the presentation-read requirement (src/core/rendering/global-transform.ts:402-415; budget raised at src/standard/rendering/entity-weight.node.ts:113-117). Once globalTransformTable is enabled, every dense placement slot retains a 48-byte previous sample, capture flags span the GlobalTransform eid capacity, and every fixed start copies the whole placement mirror and fills the flags. This includes opaque mesh-only entities never read by any CPU presentation consumer, with no VisualCorrection holders, in single-player games too. Independent GPU composition with 1024 Transform holders, no cameras/lights/audio/CPU placement calls and no corrections retained 49,152 previous-sample bytes plus 2,048 capture bytes after one tick. This is not merely an empty-pass constant. The single-player need for interpolated camera/light/transparency/audio placement is real; it does not justify retaining history for the entire opaque population. The former on-read enrollment had a first-read limitation, but the least sufficient alternative is advance enrollment of entities needing CPU presentation reads (camera/light/text/transparent candidate/audio/follower membership and a public enrollment contract for custom readers), with sparse CPU history. Enrollment must be before capture, not silently deferred until first draw. Requiring every entity's first-ever CPU read to have exact history is a newly strengthened contract/test, not a stage-2 requirement that earns the global tax. The revised 888-byte bound accounts for the chosen mechanism (including 32 bytes/eid of bound correction columns without holders); it does not justify it. My retained-entity run passed at 882.673 bytes/entity, GPU instance-data 5.333 bytes/entity; this was not the commit's reported 842.434 observation. 2. The remaining-fixed-reader audit incorrectly exempts Orbit follow (src/extras/orbit/follow.ts:72-101; src/extras/orbit/index.ts:447-450). Although the history is captured in fixed and the controller runs in simulation, followPosition explicitly interpolates the target to center the presented camera on its rendered position. It never includes VisualCorrection. Independent composed OrbitPlugin test: target Transform [3,4,5], correction translation [10,0,0], camera yaw/pitch 0 and distance 6; after step(0), composeRenderGlobalTransform(target) has x=13 while composeRenderGlobalTransform(camera) has x=3. The corrected rendered target is no longer centered; decaying correction will visibly drift relative to its follow camera. The stage explicitly includes anything following rendered entity placement. Fixing this must preserve the separation from fixed placement (for example apply the target's visual correction to the camera presentation rather than writing it into fixed target state). Existing Orbit follow tests pass because none includes VisualCorrection. Independently examined: full new commit/diff, current CPU capture/read/recovery code and correction table, production searches for GlobalTransform storage and composeGlobalTransform reads, Orbit follow implementation and callers, camera/view/ray routing, directional lighting and cascade/point-shadow consumers, transparent mesh and text sorting, spatial audio, public exports/JSDoc, retained-entity and allocation-fixture changes. Transform derivation/recovery and physics reads, explicit shadow pose writes, and Orbit's actual input/control calculations remain legitimately fixed; the follow exception above does not. Prior findings on direct camera, directional and transparency reads are repaired. Both public functions are earned: readRenderGlobalTransform is the one Xform source used by bounds/audio/lights, and composeRenderGlobalTransform is the caller-owned matrix representation used by camera/text/cascades/listener consumers, with no duplicate interpolation algorithm. Names and ownership are clear. Their existence does not require universal CPU-history retention. Validation: 57 existing tests passed across correction GPU, GPU history, transform recovery, light history, shared transparency and audio files; another 6 passed across retained-entity, Orbit follow and submission files. Independent no-consumer memory probe passed; independent corrected-Orbit-follow assertion failed as described. Temporary probes removed. I inspected the correction-enabled allocation fixture but did not rerun the long allocation oracle; the reported 0-unadmitted-byte result concerns transient allocation, not this retained-memory/fixed-copy tax. Native GPU adapter identity absent; no hardware claim.
